This page has no mathematical derivations. It continues the list from folio 109, headed 'Bereits gelesene Vorlesungen' (lecture courses already given). It is Hilbert's own running record of the courses he taught, from his Königsberg Privatdozent and professor years (1886–1895) into his early Göttingen years (from 1895). The entries are, in order:
(1) Definite integrals with Fourier series (applications to number theory);
(2) Solution of numerical equations;
(3) Invariant theory;
(4) Spherical functions (Kugelfunctionen);
(5) Algebraic numbers and functions;
(6) Foundations of geometry, with a parenthetical struck out: 'nur ausgearbeitet, nicht gelesen' (only written up, not delivered). This matches the known history: a foundations-of-geometry course was prepared but not held for lack of students, then given in Königsberg in SS 1894, as documented by Hallett & Majer;
(7) Definite integrals after Cauchy's method of integration over the complex plane;
(8) Selected chapters of invariant theory;
(9) Elliptic functions;
(10) Theory of definite integrals by Cauchy's method;
(11) On the problem of the quadrature of the circle;
(12) On the concept of number (irrationality etc.).
After this the ink and hand change to a cursive later hand, probably the Göttingen years:
(13) Theory of analytic functions of one complex variable, Parts I and II;
(14) Focal properties of curves and surfaces of second order ('Confokale' struck out and replaced by 'Fokaleigenschaften');
(15) Analytical mechanics;
(16) Foundations of Euclidean geometry (the WS 1898/99 course that led to the Festschrift 'Grundlagen der Geometrie', 1899);
(17) Calculus of variations.
The page serves as biographical and teaching documentation, not mathematics.
